From a6a890d74525510761db316787f984377535dc03 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 18:50:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] The slayer imbues, and rule 18

Batch 2 of the thirteen, first half: black mask (i) and slayer helmet (i), bought from a slayer
master with slayer points.

WHAT THE IMBUE ACTUALLY DOES, read off the wiki rather than remembered - which mattered twice:

  * it REMOVES the black mask's attack penalties. The plain mask carries magicattack -3 and
    rangeattack -1; the imbued one is +0 across the board, defences unchanged at 9/10/8/-1/9. I
    would have copied the penalties across.
  * the slayer helmet (i) ADDS magicattack +3 and rangeattack +3 and changes nothing else. Its
    defences are the plain helmet's, 30/32/27/10/30, identically.
  * on task: 15% to accuracy AND damage for ranged and magic, on top of the melee 7/6 the plain
    mask already gives. The imbue does not stack on melee - it extends the same idea sideways.

Neither item needed any art: in OSRS the imbued versions look identical, so both point at the
plain items' models.

TWO DOORS AND EIGHT CALL SITES UNTOUCHED. ~black_mask_on_task and ~slayer_helm_worn were already
the single places that answer "does this count as a mask" and "does this count as a helmet", so
teaching those two about the imbued pair carries the melee bonus, the banshee earmuffs, the
aberrant spectre nose peg, the smoke dungeon facemask and the wall beast helm across without
editing any of the eight places that ask. The imbued-only proc calls the melee one rather than
repeating its on-task test, so the two can never drift about which monsters count.

The imbue belongs to the MASK, so it survives assembly in both directions: an imbued mask makes an
imbued helmet, and taking that helmet apart hands the imbued mask back. A helmet that quietly ate
what the player paid for would be a trap.

THE PRICE IS THE ONE INVENTED NUMBER. OSRS imbues at the Nightmare Zone for 1,250,000 NMZ points,
at Soul Wars for 500 Zeal tokens, or with a Scroll of Imbuing - none of which exists in a 2006
build and none of which converts. ^slayer_imbue_cost is 1,250: OSRS's figure at a thousandth, just
above the 1,000 OSRS charges for a helmet recolour unlock, which is the nearest comparable in the
same currency. Roughly eighty tasks at the top masters. It is meant to be tuned.

RULE 18 CAME OUT OF WRITING THIS. The single-target magic cast set up $mask_num and the
multi-target proc two blocks down read it - a local does not reach the next block, the compiler
would have said so, and rs2check had no rule for it, so a full run came back clean. It does now:
rule 18 reports a $local read in a block that never declares it. Deliberately order-blind, because
use-before-declare is a different mistake and RuneScript does not scope by if/else anyway.

Its first version reported 93 findings in a repo that compiles, because it read parameters with
SIG - which matches proc, label and command and not [debugproc,clearinv](inv $inv). Three other
rules in this file have had that same first version. The selftest's clean fixture then caught the
rule's own author: the line added to prove rule 18 does not fire on correct code was itself reading
a local from another block.

Battery group 66 compares the imbued items AGAINST the plain ones rather than writing their stats
down twice, checks both doors know all four items, checks 23/20 reaches both the roll and the max
hit on every path in both combat files, and asks tools/obtainable.py whether the new items are
really obtainable.

Three of my own checks in it were too weak and three mutations survived to say so: one asked for a
substring where it should have read a set, one read only the sweep's strict list, and one had an
`or True` on the end that made it unfailable - written forty lines below a comment about checks
that cannot fail. All three are fixed. One check is documented as having NO mutation, because both
attempts at one were wrong and pretending otherwise is the thing group 65 exists to stop.

